{"chunk_id": "d34cdb1aa19a", "text": "This section recounts the unusual births that secure the continuation of the Kuru lineage. When Vyasa approaches Ambalika for a second heir, she turns pale with fear at his austere appearance, and Vyasa foretells that her son will likewise be pale\u2014Pandu, future father of the Pandavas. Later, a princess refuses another union with Vyasa and instead sends her maid, whose humility pleases the sage; their child becomes Vidura, famed for wisdom and justice. The narrative then shifts to the origin of Vidura\u2019s divine aspect: the god of Justice (Dharma) was cursed to be born among humans after the unjust punishment of the ascetic Mandavya, who was wrongly impaled by royal officers pursuing thieves. The episode explores fate, moral justice, and the complex origins of key Kuru figures.\nVyasa fathers Pandu and Vidura through niyoga, and the narrative introduces sage Mandavya whose unjust impalement leads to the curse causing Dharma\u2019s birth as Vidura.\nThemes: dynastic succession, niyoga custom, curse and karma, justice and injustice, origins of Vidura", "vector": [0.022191978991031647, -0.037884101271629333, -0.005891344975680113, -0.012836536392569542, -0.02007068693637848, 0.013054104521870613, -0.008872712031006813, -0.01842532493174076, -0.0038346434012055397, -0.0324992835521698, 0.013217281550168991, -0.0037054619751870632, 0.014101153239607811, 0.018561305478215218, -0.02110413648188114, 0.008532761596143246, 0.03826484456658363, -0.020832175388932228, 0.030622754245996475, -0.014944230206310749, -0.019553961232304573, 0.0041711945086717606, -0.008600751869380474, 0.006544049829244614, -0.030568363144993782, 0.026679327711462975, 0.024177290499210358, -0.014767455868422985, 0.008940702304244041, -0.015923287719488144, -0.02234155684709549, 0.00978377927094698, 0.020641803741455078, -0.006506655365228653, -0.03636112064123154, 0.005225041415542364, 0.003394407220184803, -0.01498502492904663, 0.02119932323694229, -0.029